# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ZZ-CLEANUP - PURGE LEFTOVER TEST MECHANICS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One-off + recurring safety net: every other suite is now self-provisioning and self-deleting (see
# tests/API/admin/delete-invariants.http, tests/API/auth/register-*.http, tests/API/profile/*.http,
# tests/API/appointments/admin-*.http), but a run that dies mid-file (killed process, network drop,
# an earlier assertion aborting before that file's own DELETE step) can still leave a disposable
# mechanic behind. This file re-lists every mechanic and deletes only the ones a fixture in this
# suite could plausibly have created and failed to clean up.
#
# Ordering caveat (verified, not assumed - same finding tests/API/_setup/ensure-test-accounts.http
# documents): the runner invokes npx httpyac tests/API/**/*.http --all ...
# (scripts/run-local-test-suite.py). Reading the pinned httpyac 6.16.7 bundle
# (httpyac/dist/index.js) shows the CLI drains the resolved file list with exactly one worker
# (Ss(e.parallel||1,...), and --parallel is never passed by the runner), so files run one at a
# time, in whatever order globby(pattern, { gitignore: true }) produced - and that base order is
# NOT stable: probing the same glob against this suite's own folders repeatedly (real requests, then
# again against instantly-failing localhost requests to rule out network jitter) returned a
# different folder order almost every run, because fast-glob/fdir walks sibling directories
# concurrently and appends results as each directory read settles. "zz-cleanup" sorting after every
# other folder name alphabetically does not guarantee httpyac resolves it last - no folder name can
# guarantee that under this mechanism, and neither can "_setup" sorting first (see that file's own
# comment for the same measurement from the other end of the suite). This file is written to not
# depend on running last: it only ever matches rows a fixture explicitly self-provisioned (see the
# filter below), so running early just finds nothing yet to purge, and running mid-suite only removes
# a fixture already abandoned by a prior, incomplete run - never one another file still in flight is
# relying on, because httpyac's single-worker draining means no other file's requests execute
# concurrently with this one.
#
# @loop support (verified, not assumed): httpyac/dist/index.js metadata parser matches @loop against
# a "for X of Y" regex and evaluates the captured iterable text as a JavaScript expression with every
# httpFile variable (including named responses such as mechanics below) spread into scope - the same
# mechanism {{seedCustomers.find(...)}} elsewhere in this suite relies on. The pinned 6.16.7 supports
# "@loop for x of y" directly, so no substitution is needed.
#
# Status assertion (per loop delete, "?? status != 500" only, never a stronger code): verified against
# AdminEndpoints.Handlers.cs (DeleteMechanicAsync / ValidateMechanicDeletionInvariantsAsync), the same
# invariants tests/API/admin/delete-invariants.http exercises directly - self-delete and admin-role are
# 403, the last-mechanic and sole-assignment guards are 422, and a concurrency race is 409. A leftover
# mechanic from another file can legitimately trip any of these (for example if it is still a sole
# appointment assignee), and that must not fail this cleanup run; only a genuine 500 should.
#
# Filter (defensive, layered): a mechanic is purged only if its email ends with "@example.test" (no
# demo mechanic - Data/DemoDataInitializer.cs seeds only @example.com addresses - and no admin/customer
# account ever uses this domain), AND its email starts with one of the prefixes literally used by this
# suite's own fixtures (grepped from tests/API/**/*.http below, not assumed from an older list), AND it
# is not the shared ARSM_TEST_MECHANIC_EMAIL account, AND it does not carry the Admin role. The prefix
# list mirrors both fixtures a file already deletes itself (defence in depth against a run dying in
# between) and fixtures a file's own validation case expects to be rejected before any row is created
# (defence against a future backend regression that would let one through):
#   matrix.                 auth/login-positive-matrix.http, login-positive-phone-matrix.http,
#                            login-negative-matrix.http, register-admin-setup-and-positive.http
#   appt.                    appointments/admin-create-update-happy.http, admin-assign-unassign.http,
#                            admin-assign-unassign-locked-state.http
#   delete.mechanic.         admin/delete-invariants.http
#   login.negmatrix.         auth/login-negative-matrix.http
#   duplicate.rules.target.  auth/register-duplicate-and-domain-rules.http
#   duplicate.phone.         auth/register-duplicate-and-domain-rules.http (rejected, no row today)
#   bad.phone.               auth/register-duplicate-and-domain-rules.http (rejected, no row today)
#   bad.expertise.           auth/register-validation-and-nonadmin-authz.http (rejected, no row today)
#   customer.should.fail.    auth/register-validation-and-nonadmin-authz.http (rejected, no row today)
#   name.                    auth/register-validation-and-nonadmin-authz.http (rejected, no row today)
#   lastname.                auth/register-validation-and-nonadmin-authz.http, field-length-validation.http (rejected)
#   middlename.              auth/register-validation-and-nonadmin-authz.http, field-length-validation.http (rejected)
#   firstname.toolong.       auth/field-length-validation.http (rejected, no row today)
#   phone.toolong.           auth/field-length-validation.http (rejected, no row today)
#   password.toolong.        auth/field-length-validation.http (rejected, no row today)
#   blocked.nonadmin.        auth/register-validation-and-nonadmin-authz.http (rejected, no row today)
#   blocked.unauth.          auth/register-admin-setup-and-positive.http (rejected, no row today)
#   profile.conflict.        profile/profile-update-conflicts.http
#   profile.delete.          profile/account-delete.http (self-deletes; kept as a backstop)
#   session.lifecycle.       auth/session-lifecycle.http

@AutoService_ApiService_HostAddress = {{$processEnv AutoService_ApiService_HostAddress}}
@WebUI_Origin = {{$processEnv ARSM_TEST_WEBUI_ORIGIN}}
@AdminEmail = {{$processEnv ARSM_TEST_ADMIN_EMAIL}}
@AdminPassword = {{$processEnv ARSM_TEST_ADMIN_PASSWORD}}
@MechanicEmail = {{$processEnv ARSM_TEST_MECHANIC_EMAIL}}


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### [Setup][Cleanup] Clear any session cookie left by a previous file (204 or 401, not asserted)
POST {{AutoService_ApiService_HostAddress}}/api/auth/logout
Origin: {{WebUI_Origin}}

###

### [Setup][Cleanup] Login as admin (200 expected)
# @name csrfSource1
POST {{AutoService_ApiService_HostAddress}}/api/auth/login
Origin: {{WebUI_Origin}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{AdminEmail}}",
  "password": "{{AdminPassword}}"
}

{{
  exports.CsrfToken = response.headers['set-cookie']?.find(c => c.startsWith('autoservice_csrf='))?.match(/^autoservice_csrf=([^;]+)/)?.[1];
}}

?? status == 200

###

### [Setup][Cleanup] List every mechanic (200 expected)
# @name mechanics
GET {{AutoService_ApiService_HostAddress}}/api/admin/mechanics

?? status == 200

###

### [Cleanup] Delete every leftover test-fixture mechanic still matching the filter above
# @loop for leftoverMechanic of mechanics.filter(m => !m.isAdmin && m.email.toLowerCase() !== MechanicEmail.toLowerCase() && m.email.toLowerCase().endsWith("@example.test") && ["matrix.","appt.","delete.mechanic.","login.negmatrix.","duplicate.rules.target.","duplicate.phone.","bad.phone.","bad.expertise.","customer.should.fail.","name.","lastname.","middlename.","firstname.toolong.","phone.toolong.","password.toolong.","blocked.nonadmin.","blocked.unauth.","profile.conflict.","profile.delete.","session.lifecycle."].some(p => m.email.toLowerCase().startsWith(p)))
DELETE {{AutoService_ApiService_HostAddress}}/api/admin/mechanics/{{leftoverMechanic.personId}}
Origin: {{WebUI_Origin}}
X-CSRF-Token: {{CsrfToken}}

# Never a stronger assertion here (see the header comment): DeleteMechanicAsync legitimately answers
# 403 (self/admin-role), 404 (already gone), 409 (concurrency) or 422 (last-mechanic/sole-assignment)
# for a leftover this file did not create context for, and none of those should fail this cleanup run.
?? status != 500
